     "replacement": "Modified growth and decay problems usually involve finding $k$.\n\n$$\\text{if }\\frac{dQ}{dt}=k(Q-P),\\quad\\text{then }Q=P+Ae^{kt}$$\n\n1. Write $Q=P+Ae^{kt}$, where $P$ is the limiting value and $P+A$ is the initial value.\n2. Substitute the given values and solve for $k$. Leave $k$ in exact form.\n3. Rewrite the equation with $P$, $A$ and $k$.\n4. Solve for what the question asks.\n\nWhen you differentiate or solve for $t$, keep writing $k$ instead of its exact value to avoid a long logarithm on every line.",
